es arousal and cognitive recovery — dual benefit)\n   - **Valproic acid** if seizures co-occur — mood stabilization and seizure control\n   - **Buspirone** 10-15 mg TID for anxiety-driven agitation\n   - **Trazodone** 50-100 mg QHS for sleep fragmentation\n\n4. **Agents to AVOID in TBI**:\n   - Typical antipsychotics (haloperidol) — impair recovery\n   - Benzodiazepines — paradoxical disinhibition, impair cognition and neuroplasticity\n   - Anticholinergics — worsen confusion\n\n5. **PRN Acute Agitation** (when non-pharmacological strategies fail):\n   - Olanzapine 2.5-5 mg IM (preferred over haloperidol if antipsychotic needed acutely)\n   - Short-term use only with plan to taper\n\n**Restraint Alternatives**:\n- Physical restraints are **last resort** and should be avoided — they increase agitation, cause injury, and violate patient autonomy\n- Alternatives: 1:1 sitter, enclosed low bed, padded side rails, alarm-equipped wheelchair, secured unit with wanderguard system\n- If restraints are absolutely necessary for imminent safety threat: use least restrictive method, physician order required, reassess every 1-2 hours, document ongoing need',
